{
  "dom_id": "step-71920",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We know how to find $2^{-1}.$ But what about $2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots$?\n\nTo work out ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},$ all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}})$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+2}}})$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1}{2\\times 2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example.",
